Post-Concussion Symptom Cluster
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Persistent headache or dizziness post-mild TBI | Cognitive slowing or functional brain fog | Structurally normal head CT/MRI

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-016
Reason for Visit: Acute worsening of severe headache, confusion, and dizziness following a recent minor head injury, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude an evolving subdural hematoma.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate escalating neurological symptoms 48 hours post-head trauma. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out delayed intracranial hemorrhage or expanding structural lesions, preventing irreversible neurological injury or death prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My head hasn't stopped throbbing since I bumped it two days ago, and every time the lights are on I feel completely sick and confused.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents following a minor head strike against a low doorframe 48 hours ago. There is a persistent mild headache that has subjectively worsened tonight. The patient reports dizziness and subjective balance issues when mobilizing. The spouse notes impaired short-term memory, concentration difficulties, and a vague fogginess in the patient's thought processes. The patient displays mild irritability and emotional lability, alongside profound sensitivity to light and noise. Sleep disturbances are marked by fragmented rest. Fatigue is notably exacerbated by even mild mental exertion.
Functional Status: Currently requiring assistance for basic activities due to severe photophobia and dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk due to balance issues and dark environment.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/80 mmHg | HR: 76 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 14 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Patient appears uncomfortable, wearing sunglasses indoors, and speaking slowly. Systemic assessment incidentally identifies an overarching loss of independent ADLs, decreased gait speed, and generalized exhaustion compounding the primary neurological complaint.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive neurological examination reveals an entirely normal presentation without focal motor or sensory deficits. Cranial nerves II-XII are grossly intact. Pupils are equal, round, and reactive to light. Gait is slow but non-ataxic. Pronator drift is absent. Finger-to-nose testing is normal. Head and neck palpation reveals mild suboccipital muscular tension but no bony step-offs.
Environmental Observations: Room is heavily darkened with all electronic devices turned off.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient took paracetamol with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Concussion Symptom Cluster - Status: Exacerbated by environmental sensory overload and poor sleep.
Differentials:

Subdural Hematoma - Ruled out clinically due to the complete absence of focal neurological deficits, equal pupillary response, and preserved level of consciousness.

Migraine - Ruled out as the primary acute cause due to the distinct onset strictly following physical head trauma and lack of characteristic visual aura.

Cervicogenic Headache - Ruled out as the sole etiology due to the prominent cognitive fogginess and significant emotional lability, which point to central mild traumatic brain injury rather than isolated cervical strain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s presentation mandated immediate after-hours evaluation due to escalating neurological symptoms following a head injury, raising a high index of suspicion for delayed intracranial hemorrhage. Telephone triage appropriately flagged the severe headache and confusion as potential red flags for an expanding subdural hematoma. Rigorous cranial nerve and somatic neurological examination firmly established the absence of focal deficits or signs of increased intracranial pressure. This definitive physical assessment allowed for safe home-based conservative management, entirely avoiding a chaotic and highly distressing late-night emergency department transfer for an unindicated head CT.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated strict cognitive rest protocols. The expected investigation results include a normal acute head CT without intracranial hemorrhage or structural lesion that would assist in confirming the diagnosis if symptoms atypically progress and imaging is later required.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce central nervous system strain, adhere strictly to the "20-20-20 rule" (every 20 minutes, look at something 20 feet away for 20 seconds) during any necessary visually demanding tasks; minimizing sustained visual focus will directly reduce your headache intensity and photophobia.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: Use physical handholds when walking to the bathroom in the dark.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours for a formal return-to-work and driving assessment.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops repeated forceful vomiting, unequal pupil size, sudden weakness on one side of the body, or becomes increasingly drowsy and difficult to wake.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; mild traumatic brain injuries are dynamic and symptoms can evolve over days to weeks.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves gradual, linear improvement over 7 to 14 days, heavily dependent on strict cognitive and physical rest in the initial phase.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your caregiver must seek immediate emergency medical attention if you become completely confused, start vomiting repeatedly, or experience a seizure.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: We are not sending you for a CT scan tonight because your neurological exam is completely normal, and unnecessary radiation will not change the requirement for strict brain rest.
Actionable Steps: Eliminate all screen time (phones, televisions, computers) for the next 48 hours to prevent severe symptom exacerbations.

